count = Math.round((139.96 - 20.99) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(118.97 * 100) / 100 = 11897 / 100 = 118.97\n- taxAmount = Math.round(118.97 * 0.1 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(11.897 * 100) / 100 = 1190 / 100 = 11.90\n- total = 118.97 + 11.90 = 130.87\n\nBut the test expects 130.86. Let me trace through what the test comment says:\n```\n// subtotal = 3*29.99 + 49.99 = 139.96\n// discount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n// after discount = 118.966\n// tax = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\n// total = 130.8626 -> rounded to 130.86\n```\n\nSo the test expects minimal rounding - just one final rounding to 2 decimal places. The current code rounds at each step (discount, afterDiscount, taxAmount) which causes accumulated rounding errors.\n\nThe fix should be to NOT round intermediate values. Only round the final total. Or specifically:\n- subtotal - no rounding needed (already fine)\n- discountAmount = subtotal * discountPercent / 100 but NOT rounded\n- afterDiscount = subtotal - discountAmount (NOT rounded)\n- taxAmount = afterDiscount * taxRate (NOT rounded)\n- total = Math.round((afterDiscount + taxAmount) * 100) / 100\n\nWait, let me think more carefully. The test expects:\n- discount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n- after discount = 118.966\n- tax = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\n- total = 130.8626 -> rounded to 130.86\n\nSo the round-to-100 approach (multiply by 100, round, divide by 100) should only be applied to the final total.\n\nLet me also check the \"penny-edge amounts\" test:\n```\n// subtotal = 33.33\n// discount = 33.33 * 0.05 = 1.6665\n// after discount = 31.6635\n// tax = 31.6635 * 0.07 = 2.216445\n// total = 33.879945 -> 33.88\n```\n\nWith current code:\n- subtotal = 33.33\n- discountAmount = Math.round(33.33 * 0.05 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(1.6665 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(166.65) / 100 = 167 / 100 = 1.67\n- afterDiscount = Math.round((33.33 - 1.67) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(31.66 * 100) / 100 = 3166 / 100 = 31.66\n- taxAmount = Math.round(31.66 * 0.07 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(2.2162 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(221.62) / 100 = 222 / 100 = 2.22\n- total = 31.66 + 2.22 = 33.88\n\nHmm, this one happens to pass with the current code! But the first test fails.\n\nSo the fix is to not round intermediate values and only round the final total.\n\nLet me think again:\n```\nconst subtotal = items.reduce((sum, item) => sum + item.unitPrice * item.quantity, 0);\nconst discountAmount = subtotal * (discountPercent / 100);\nconst afterDiscount = subtotal - discountAmount;\nconst taxAmount = afterDiscount * taxRate;\nconst total = Math.round((afterDiscount + taxAmount) * 100) / 100;\n```\n\nFor the failing test:\n- subtotal = 139.96\n- discountAmount = 139.96 * 0.15 = 20.994\n- afterDiscount = 139.96 - 20.994 = 118.966\n- taxAmount = 118.966 * 0.1 = 11.8966\n- total = Math.round((118.966 + 11.8966) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(130.8626 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(13086.26) / 100 = 13086 / 100 = 130.86 ✓\n\nFor the penny-edge test:\n- subtotal = 33.33\n- discountAmount = 33.33 * 0.05 = 1.6665\n- afterDiscount = 33.33 - 1.6665 = 31.6635\n- taxAmount = 31.6635 * 0.07 = 2.216445\n- total = Math.round((31.6635 + 2.216445) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(33.879945 * 100) / 100 = Math.round(3387.9945) / 100 = 3388 / 100 = 33.88 ✓\n\nFor the 10% discount test:\n- subtotal = 100\n- discountAmount = 100 * 0.10 = 10\n- afterDiscount = 90\n- taxAmount = 90 * 0.08 = 7.2\n- total = Math.round((90 + 7.2) * 100) / 100 = Math.round(9720) / 100 = 97.2 ✓\n\nFor the no-discount/no-tax test:\n- subtotal = 100\n- discountAmount = 0\n- afterDiscount = 100\n- taxAmount = 0\n- total = Math.round(100 * 100) / 100 = 100 ✓\n\nGreat, all tests pass with this approach.\n\n### Bug 3: `OrderService.cancelOrder()` - Event data type issue\n**File:** `src/orders/service.ts` line 75-83\n**Test:** `cancels a confirmed order` - `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')`\n\nThe error says `order.items` is undefined when `cancelOrder` tries to access `order.items.map(...)`. But looking at line 78, `cancelOrder` does `order.items.map((i) => ...)`. Looking at the test, when it calls `cancelOrder`, the order was created via `createOrder` which does set items.\n\nWait - the error says `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'map')` at `src/orders/service.ts:78:26`. Let me re-read line 78:\n```\nitems: order.items.map((i) => ({\n```\n\nSo `order.items` is undefined. But the `createOrder` creates the order with items... Oh wait! Look at the `createOrder` method:\n\n```typescript\nasync createOrder(...): Promise<Order> {\n    this.repo.create({\n      id,\n      customerId,\n      items: [...items],\n      status: 'pending',\n      total: 0,\n      discount,\n      taxRate,\n      createdAt: Date.now(),\n      updatedAt: Date.now(),\n    });\n\n    const event: OrderCreatedEvent = {\n      orderId: id,\n      items: items.map((i) => ({\n        productId: i.productId,\n        quantity: i.quantity,\n        unitPrice: i.unitPrice,\n      })),\n      customerId,\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    };\n\n    await this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED, event);\n\n    // Charge payment and confirm\n    const payment = await this.billingService.chargePayment(\n      id,\n      items,\n      discount,\n      taxRate,\n      'card'\n    );\n\n    this.repo.update(id, {\n      status: 'confirmed',\n      total: payment.amount,\n    });\n\n    await this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CONFIRMED, {\n      orderId: id,\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    });\n\n    return this.repo.findById(id)!;\n  }\n```\n\nWhen `createOrder` is called, it first creates the order in the repo, then emits `order.created`, then charges payment, then updates the order to confirmed.\n\nBUT - during `eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED, event)`, the `InventoryService.handleOrderCreated` is called. If this handler throws (e.g., insufficient stock), the error propagates up and `createOrder` throws. In that case, the order is already created in the repo but never gets updated to 'confirmed'.\n\nWait, but the test for `cancels a confirmed order` first creates the order with sufficient stock:\n```\ninventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\nawait orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n    { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 2, unitPrice: 10 },\n]);\n```\n\nSo the order should be created fine. And then `cancelOrder` is called. Let me re-read `cancelOrder`:\n\n```typescript\nasync cancelOrder(id: string, reason: string): Promise<Order | undefined> {\n    const order = this.repo.findById(id);\n    if (!order) return undefined;\n    if (order.status !== 'confirmed') return undefined;\n\n    // Emit cancellation event, then update status\n    await this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED, {\n      orderId: id,\n      reason,\n      items: order.items.map((i) => ({\n        productId: i.productId,\n        quantity: i.quantity,\n      })),\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\n    });\n\n    this.repo.update(id, { status: 'cancelled' });\n    return this.repo.findById(id);\n}\n```\n\nHmm, `order.items` should be defined because `findById` returns a copy that should have items... \n\nWait! Let me look at the `update` bug. Since `createOrder` calls:\n```typescript\nthis.repo.update(id, {\n    status: 'confirmed',\n    total: payment.amount,\n});\n```\n\nAnd the `update` method does:\n```typescript\nconst updated = { ...updates, id, updatedAt: Date.now() } as Order;\n```\n\nThis replaces the entire order with JUST the updates + id + updatedAt! So after `createOrder` updates the order to 'confirmed', the stored order becomes `{ status: 'confirmed', total: payment.amount, id: 'O1', updatedAt: Date.now() }` — losing `customerId`, `items`, `discount`, `taxRate`, `createdAt`, etc.\n\nSo when `cancelOrder` later does `order.items.map(...)`, `order.items` is undefined because it was lost in the update!\n\nThis is the root cause of multiple failures. Fixing the `OrderRepository.update()` to preserve existing fields should fix:\n\n1. `cancels a confirmed order` - `order.items` will exist\n2. `refuses to cancel a non-confirmed order` - same\n3. `refunds a cancelled order within 30 days` - same\n4. `OrderRepository > updates order preserves other fields` - directly tests this\n5. `allows refund on exactly day 30` - the order probably has proper data now\n\nBut wait, let me also check the refund test for \"allows refund on exactly day 30\":\n```typescript\nit('allows refund on exactly day 30', async () => {\n    inventoryService.setStock('SKU-1', 10);\n    await orderService.createOrder('O1', 'C1', [\n      { productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 1, unitPrice: 100 },\n    ]);\n    const thirtyDaysMs = 30 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000;\n    orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs });\n    await orderService.cancelOrder('O1', 'late cancel');\n    const refunded = await orderService.refundOrder('O1');\n    expect(refunded?.status).toBe('refunded');\n});\n```\n\nWith the bug in `update`, after `orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs })`, the order loses all fields except `createdAt`, `id`, `updatedAt`. So `order.status` becomes undefined, and `cancelOrder` returns undefined because `order.status !== 'confirmed'`. Then the test proceeds to call `refundOrder('O1')` which also returns undefined because the order isn't in the right status.\n\nWait actually, the test calls `orderRepo.update('O1', ...)` directly to backdate the order. But since `update` has the bug, after this call, the order is just `{ createdAt: ..., id: 'O1', updatedAt: ... }`. Then `cancelOrder('O1')` checks `order.status !== 'confirmed'` - but `order` after the update call is `findById` which returns a copy... Let me trace more carefully.\n\n`cancelOrder('O1', 'late cancel')`:\n1. `const order = this.repo.findById(id)` - This returns a copy of whatever's in the store.\n2. But wait - after `orderRepo.update('O1', { createdAt: Date.now() - thirtyDaysMs })` in the test, the order in the store is now `{ createdAt: backdated, id: 'O1', updatedAt: now }`. So `order.status` is undefined (since the existing fields were lost in the update).\n3. `if (order.status !== 'confirmed') return undefined` → returns undefined\n\nThen `refundOrder('O1')`:\n1. `const order = this.repo.findById(id)` - same broken order data\n2. `if (!order) return undefined` - no, order exists\n3. `if (order.status !== 'cancelled') return undefined` - status is undefined, not 'cancelled', returns undefined\n\nSo the test gets undefined and `expect(undefined?.status).toBe('refunded')` fails because it's actually checking `undefined`.\n\nFixing the `update` method should fix ALL these issues!\n\n### Bug 4: Inventory stock release - `handleOrderCancelled` checks for `status`\n**File:** `src/inventory/service.ts` line 50\n**Test:** `releases stock on order.cancelled`\n\n```typescript\nif (eventWithItems.status !== 'cancelled') return;\n```\n\nThis checks that the event has `status: 'cancelled'`. Let me see what the `cancelOrder` emits:\n\n```typescript\nawait this.eventBus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED, {\n    orderId: id,\n    reason,\n    items: order.items.map((i) => ({\n        productId: i.productId,\n        quantity: i.quantity,\n    })),\n    timestamp: Date.now(),\n});\n```\n\nThe emitted event does NOT include a `status` field! So `eventWithItems.status` is undefined, and `undefined !== 'cancelled'` is true, so the handler returns early without releasing stock. \n\nBut wait, looking at the inventory test \"releases stock on order.cancelled\":\n```typescript\nawait bus.emit(ORDER_EVENTS.CANCELLED, {\n    orderId: 'O1',\n    reason: 'changed mind',\n    status: 'cancelled',    // <-- has status field!\n    items: [{ productId: 'SKU-1', quantity: 3 }],\n    timestamp: Date.now(),\n});\n```\n\nThe test directly emits the event WITH a status field, so it works when tested directly via the bus.\n\nBut when the OrderService emits the cancellation event (via `cancelOrder`), it does NOT include the `status` field. So the inventory handler doesn't release stock.\n\nThe fix: remove the `status` check from `handleOrderCancelled`, or add `status: 'cancelled'` to the event emitted by `cancelOrder`.\n\nLooking at the tests for OrderService:\n- `releases stock when order is cancelled` calls `orderService.cancelOrder('O1', ...)` and expects stock to be released\n- Without the status field in the event, this doesn't work\n\nThe cleanest fix is to just remove the `status` check in `handleOrderCancelled`. The event `order.cancelled` implies cancellation — there's no need for a redundant status check.\n\n### Bug 5: `BillingService.handleRefund` - Not bound properly\n**File:** `src/billing/service.ts` line 25\n**Test:** multiple billing tests failing with `Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'payments')`\n\n```typescript\nregisterHandlers(): void {\n    this.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.STOCK_RESERVED, this.handleStockReserved.bind(this));\n    this.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.REFUNDED, this.handleRefund);  // <-- NOT bound!\n}\n```\n\n`this.handleStockReserved` is correctly bound with `.bind(this)`, but `this.handleRefund` is NOT. So when the event bus calls `this.handleRefund(data)` in the handler, `this` is undefined (or the global object), and `this.payments` fails.\n\nThe fix: add `.bind(this)` to `this.handleRefund`.\n\n### Summary of Bugs Found:\n\n1. **OrderRepository.update()** - Doesn't preserve existing fields (spreads only `updates`, not `existing`)\n2. **calculateOrderTotal()** - Over-rounding intermediate values (discount, afterDiscount, taxAmount all rounded individually, then total is rounded again)\n3. **InventoryService.handleOrderCancelled()** - Unnecessary `status` check prevents stock release when OrderService emits cancellation without status field\n4. **BillingService.registerHandlers()** - `this.handleRefund` registered without `.bind(this)`, loses `this` context\n\nLet me now fix all of these.","signature":"e91bf98d-fffc-4b4c-95f4-9b263bfffcec"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":519