fine.\n\nWait, the EventBus uses `globalHandlers` but handlers are accumulated from ALL previous tests. Each `beforeEach` creates `bus = new EventBus()` and `service = new InventoryService(repo, bus)` and calls `service.registerHandlers()`. But `registerHandlers()` calls `this.eventBus.on(ORDER_EVENTS.CREATED, this.handleOrderCreated.bind(this))` on the NEW event bus, which pushes to the global handlers.\n\nBut `this` in the bound function refers to the NEW service instance with the NEW repo. So each test gets its own handler, and the handler references the correct repo for that test's `beforeEach`. That should be fine...\n\nUNLESS the old handler from the previous test is still in `globalHandlers`! Let me trace:\n\nTest 1: \"reserves stock on order.created\"\n- `beforeEach` creates bus1, repo1, service1, calls registerHandlers\n- globalHandlers has [handleOrderCreated1, handleOrderCancelled1]\n- Runs test, emits CREATED -> handleOrderCreated1 fires against repo1\n\nTest 2: \"throws when stock is insufficient\"\n- `beforeEach` creates bus2, repo2, service2, calls registerHandlers\n- globalHandlers has [handleOrderCreated1, handleOrderCancelled1, handleOrderCreated2, handleOrderCancelled2]\n- Runs test, emits CREATED -> BOTH handleOrderCreated1 AND handleOrderCreated2 fire!\n- But handleOrderCreated1 uses repo1 which has no stock set...\n\nThat's the bug! Each `beforeEach` registers NEW handlers without clearing the old ones. So multiple handlers accumulate for the same event, and they all fire. Old handlers reference old repos that have no stock.\n\nWait, but the EventBus has `clear()` method that clears `globalHandlers`. Each `beforeEach` doesn't call `bus.clear()`, so handlers accumulate.\n\nBut actually, I need to check if EventBus.clear() is called anywhere... Let me look at tests.\n\nActually, wait - the problem might also be that old EventBus instances from previous tests might not have their handlers removed. But that would affect ALL tests, not just these tw